# DESCRIPTION

cdns-gpio drives one of the Sky1's seven Cadence GPIO controllers, `CIXH1002` (the FCH's first, at `0x04120000`) and `CIXH1003` (the others), by its record:

```text
driver cdns-gpio
class gpio
match acpi CIXH1002
match acpi CIXH1003
```

A controller has up to 32 lines and one interrupt.
The count comes from its address: the four FCH controllers (`0x04120000`, `0x04130000`, `0x04140000`, `0x04150000`) have 32, 32, 32 and 17, and the three S5 controllers (`0x16004000`, `0x16005000`, `0x16006000`) 32, 10 and 10.
An ACPI device's `GpioIo` and `GpioInt` connections name a controller and pins by their number in that controller, not in the chip: pin 6 of the S5's first controller is the line the Orange Pi 6 Plus's embedded controller raises.
devmgr(8) gives the controller's tree to that device's driver at `/dev/provider/<service>`, and its `/dev/acpi/links` gives the connection's pin and flags (bit 0 active low) as the cells a command takes.
A devicetree provider such as k1-gpio(3) numbers its pins by bank and pin; this one has a single number, so a command has one cell fewer.

At start the driver turns the controller's clock on through the SCMI agent, masks every line's interrupt, clears what was pending, and only then lets the controller's interrupt through.
The controller's reset is the firmware's: it isn't touched, as one reset serves all four FCH controllers.
A line no command names keeps its direction, output, level and function as firmware left them.

A command takes a line over from any bypass (its pad passed to another function): only its bit changes, in each register.
An output is set to its level first, its driver enabled, and made an output last, so it never drives the other level.
A watched line is made an input, masked while its trigger is set, cleared of an old interrupt, and unmasked last.
The five triggers are `rising`, `falling`, `both` (the default), `high` and `low`, and each sets all three of the controller's trigger registers.
The interrupt, served on a thread of its own and every 50 ms against a lost one, reads the status once, clears what it finds, and counts one for each watched line that had one.
A level line that has fired is masked, as nothing here can service its source: it is watched again to arm it.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, and at `/dev/provider/<service>` in the drivers of the devices that link to it.

: `ctl`
  Takes a command a line, `gpio <pin> <flags> on|off|input|watch [<trigger>]`: the pin driven to its active level, to the other, made an input, or watched, numbers in decimal or `0x` hexadecimal.
  A trigger follows only `watch`.
  A line it can't parse, a pin the controller doesn't have, or a `watch` with no interrupt to serve it fails the write as invalid, and the driver logs which.
: `events`
  Read only.
  A line for each watched pin, `gpio <pin> <flags> active|inactive edges <n>`, its level now and the interrupts that found its trigger on it; `none` with none watched.
  A channel's first read answers at once; each later one waits until the text differs from what that channel read last.
: `status`
  Read only.
  `gpios <n>`; where the registers don't read as a controller's, `no response`; a line for each pin a command has named, `pin 6 input level 1 watched rising edges 3`, with `bypassed` where its pad is passed to another function and `masked` where a level line has fired; then `commands <n>, failed <n>`, `last failure: <command>: <why>`, and `interrupts <n> polls <n>`, or `no interrupt: no line can be watched`.

